    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "9,500 / 8,500"
      ],
      [
        "Peak Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "12,500 / 11,200"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time at 50% Load",
        "8 hr gas (6.6 gal) / 7 hr propane (20 lb)"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "2x 5-20R GFCI duplex, L14-30R 30A, 14-50R 50A"
      ],
      [
        "Dry Weight",
        "211 lb"
      ],
      [
        "Starting",
        "Electric, remote key fob, recoil"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Smallest propane penalty in the big-portable class: 8,500 running watts on LP",
      "Both a 30A L14-30R and a 50A 14-50R, so it fits either size of transfer switch",
      "Remote key-fob start, handy in freezing rain",
      "Westinghouse lists it as transfer switch ready",
      "6.6-gallon tank with a built-in fuel gauge"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The base WGen9500DF has no CO sensor; the WGen9500DFc version adds one",
      "211 lb dry, so it lives on its wheels",
      "Rated at 74 dBA, which neighbors will hear"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The dual fuel generator to buy if you want real whole-house-adjacent output on either fuel and a 50-amp outlet ready for a proper hookup.",
    "description": "The WGen9500DF earns the top spot because it loses less on propane than most rivals. Westinghouse rates it at 9,500 running and 12,500 peak watts on gasoline, and 8,500 running and 11,200 peak on propane, a drop of roughly 10 percent where many open-frame units give up 15 percent or more. That matters, because the whole reason to buy dual fuel is to run on propane when gas stations are dark. The panel carries two GFCI duplex outlets, a 30-amp L14-30R and a 50-amp 14-50R, so it works with either a 30-amp manual transfer switch or a 50-amp inlet and interlock. Westinghouse lists run time at about 8 hours on gasoline and 7 hours on a 20 lb propane cylinder at half load, from a 6.6-gallon tank with a gauge. Electric start with a remote key fob is a real convenience. The trade-offs are 211 lb of dry weight and 74 dBA of noise. The standard model has no carbon monoxide shutoff; if that matters to you, and it should, look for the WGen9500DFc, which adds CO sensing."
  },
  {
    "id": "champion-9200-dual-fuel",
    "name": "Champion 9200-Watt Dual Fuel Portable Generator with CO Shield (201302)",
    "category": "9,200W running / 11,500W starting on gas, CO Shield",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running / Starting Watts (Gas)",
        "9,200 / 11,500"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time at 50% Load",
        "10 hr gas / 5 hr propane"
      ],
      [
        "Gas Tank",
        "7.7 gal"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "2x 5-20R GFCI duplex, L14-30R 30A, 14-50R (38A max)"
      ],
      [
        "Engine",
        "459cc Champion OHV"
      ],
      [
        "Noise",
        "74 dBA"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "CO Shield shuts the engine off if carbon monoxide builds up",
      "Longest gasoline run time here at half load, about 10 hours",
      "Fuel selector dial switches between gas and propane",
      "30A and 50A outlets cover either transfer switch size",
      "Cold Start Technology for winter outages"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Propane output is lower than the gasoline rating; read the unit label",
      "About 5 hours per 20 lb cylinder at half load means a lot of tank swaps",
      "Its 50A outlet is limited to 38 amps"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best value dual fuel generator for most suburban houses, with CO shutoff built in.",
    "description": "This is the dual fuel generator most people should price first, and it is the same 9,200-watt class Champion that our home outage guide ranks as its top portable. Champion rates it at 11,500 starting and 9,200 running watts on gasoline from a 459cc engine, enough for a refrigerator, a freezer, a sump pump, a gas furnace blower, lights and Wi-Fi together, with room for a window air conditioner if you stagger start-ups. Like every open-frame dual fuel unit, it makes less on propane, so build your load list from the propane figure on the label, not the headline number. The 7.7-gallon tank gives about 10 hours at half load on gasoline; on propane, Champion lists about 5 hours at half load, so plan on several 20 lb cylinders a day. CO Shield shuts the engine off if carbon monoxide accumulates around it, a backup to good placement rather than a substitute. The panel has GFCI household outlets, a 30-amp L14-30R and a 14-50R that Champion caps at 38 amps. Check the model number before you buy: Champion also sells gasoline-only 9200-watt units."
  },
  {
    "id": "duromax-xp13000eh",
    "name": "DuroMax XP13000EH Dual Fuel Portable Generator",
    "category": "10,500W running gas / 9,975W propane, 500cc",
    "badge": "Most Power",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "10,500 / 9,975"
      ],
      [
        "Peak Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "13,000 / 12,350"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time at 50% Load",
        "8.5 hr gas / 6.5 hr propane"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "2x 120V 20A GFCI, 120V 30A twist lock, 120/240V 30A twist lock, 120/240V 50A"
      ],
      [
        "Engine",
        "500cc OHV"
      ],
      [
        "Weight",
        "248 lb (DuroMax listing)"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Highest propane output on this list: 9,975 running watts",
      "50A 120/240V outlet for a large inlet and interlock setup",
      "All-copper alternator windings and all-metal construction",
      "Enough surge for a 240V well pump alongside household loads",
      "Push-button electric start"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "248 lb; it needs a hard path to its operating spot",
      "No CO shutoff on the EH version",
      "Very thirsty at heavy load on either fuel"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The portable to buy when you need close to 10,000 watts on propane, not just on gasoline.",
    "description": "The XP13000EH is as much generator as most homeowners can still call portable. DuroMax rates it at 13,000 peak and 10,500 running watts on gasoline, and 12,350 peak and 9,975 running watts on propane, the strongest propane numbers here by a clear margin. That makes it the pick for houses with a 240V well pump, an electric water heater you want to run in short bursts, or a smaller central air system, after an electrician checks its starting draw. The 500cc engine and copper windings are built for heavy loads, and the panel carries both 30-amp twist-lock outlets and a 120/240V 50-amp outlet, so it suits a 50-amp inlet and interlock. DuroMax lists about 8.5 hours on gasoline and 6.5 hours on propane at half load. The costs are physical. DuroMax lists it at 248 lb, it is rated at 74 dB, and at heavy load it burns through a 20 lb cylinder fast enough that a 100 lb tank or several 40 lb tanks make more sense. The EH has no CO shutoff; DuroMax sells CO Alert versions under other model numbers."
  },
  {
    "id": "generac-gp7500e-df",
    "name": "Generac GP7500E Dual Fuel Portable Generator with COsense",
    "category": "7,500W running gas / 6,800W LP, COsense, PowerRush",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Size",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / LP)",
        "7,500 / 6,800"
      ],
      [
        "Starting Watts (Gas / LP)",
        "9,400 / 8,500"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time at 50% Load",
        "10 hr gas (7.9 gal) / 5 hr LP (20 lb)"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "2x 5-20R GFCI duplex, L14-30R 30A"
      ],
      [
        "Weight",
        "204 lb"
      ],
      [
        "Safety",
        "COsense CO shutoff"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "COsense shuts the engine down if carbon monoxide builds up",
      "PowerRush, which Generac says adds over 30% more starting capacity",
      "Fuel switches with a dial",
      "Transfer switch ready through the L14-30R",
      "Generac's large dealer and parts network"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No 50A outlet, so it pairs with a 30-amp switch or inlet only",
      "Still 204 lb despite the smaller output",
      "About 5 hours on a 20 lb LP cylinder at half load"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible mid-size dual fuel generator for houses with gas heat that need essentials, not everything.",
    "description": "Not every house needs 9,000-plus watts, and the GP7500E Dual Fuel is the pick for the many that do not. Generac rates it at 7,500 running and 9,400 starting watts on gasoline, and 6,800 running and 8,500 starting on LP. That is enough for a refrigerator, a freezer, a sump pump, a gas furnace blower, lights and a window air conditioner with sensible staggering. Generac's PowerRush feature is designed to boost starting capacity for motor loads, which helps with pumps and compressors. COsense monitors carbon monoxide around the unit and shuts it down if levels climb. Generac lists up to 10 hours at half load on gasoline from a 7.9-gallon tank, and 5 hours on a 20 lb LP cylinder. The panel has two GFCI duplex outlets and an L14-30R, and Generac describes it as transfer switch ready, so it matches a standard 30-amp manual transfer switch or 30-amp inlet. What you give up is the 50-amp outlet and the extra headroom of the 9,500-watt class, and it is still 204 lb. If your load list fits under about 6,500 watts on propane, it is the simpler buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-igen4500df",
    "name": "Westinghouse iGen4500DF Dual Fuel Inverter Generator",
    "category": "3,700W running gas / 3,330W propane inverter, TT-30R",
    "badge": "Best Dual Fuel Inverter",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "3,700 / 3,330"
      ],
      [
        "Peak Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "4,500 / 4,050"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time at 25% Load",
        "18 hr gas (3.4 gal) / 14 hr propane (20 lb)"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "1x 5-20R duplex, TT-30R 30A RV, 2x USB"
      ],
      [
        "Noise",
        "As low as 52 dBA"
      ],
      [
        "Dry Weight",
        "98 lb"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Clean inverter power, under 3% THD per Westinghouse",
      "Rated as low as 52 dBA, far quieter than open frames",
      "14 hours on a 20 lb propane cylinder at quarter load",
      "Electric, remote fob and recoil start",
      "Parallel capable with another iGen"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "120V only, and Westinghouse lists it as not transfer switch ready",
      "Not enough output for a 240V well pump or central air",
      "98 lb dry is heavy for a 3,700-watt unit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The quiet, fuel-efficient dual fuel choice for essentials and RV duty, not for wiring into a panel.",
    "description": "If you want dual fuel without the noise of an open-frame engine, the iGen4500DF is the one to look at. Westinghouse rates it at 3,700 running and 4,500 peak watts on gasoline, and 3,330 running and 4,050 peak on propane. Because it is an inverter, the engine throttles down at light load, which is why the spec sheet lists 18 hours at quarter load on its 3.4-gallon tank and 14 hours on a standard 20 lb propane cylinder. That is the best propane economy on this list by a wide margin. Output is clean, with Westinghouse listing under 3 percent total harmonic distortion, and the operating noise is rated as low as 52 dBA. The limits are important. It is 120V only, with a 5-20R duplex, a TT-30R RV outlet and two USB ports, and Westinghouse's own sheet marks it as not transfer switch ready. So it is a run-extension-cords-to-essentials generator, or an RV generator, not a panel-feeding one. It weighs 98 lb dry. It can be paralleled with another iGen using Westinghouse's cord if you outgrow one."
  },
  {
    "id": "duromax-xp13000hxt",
    "name": "DuroMax XP13000HXT Tri Fuel Portable Generator with CO Alert",
    "category": "10,500W gas / 9,500W propane / 8,500W natural gas",
    "badge": "Best Tri Fuel",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / Propane / NG)",
        "10,500 / 9,500 / 8,500"
      ],
      [
        "Peak Watts (Gas / Propane / NG)",
        "13,000 / 12,000 / 10,000"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time at 50% Load",
        "8.5 hr gas / 6.5 hr propane"
      ],
      [
        "Engine",
        "500cc OHV"
      ],
      [
        "Starting",
        "Push button, plus remote fob per retailer listings"
      ],
      [
        "Safety",
        "CO Alert shutdown"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Runs on gasoline, propane or a natural gas line",
      "CO Alert shuts the engine down when carbon monoxide builds",
      "Big 120/240V output for well pumps and larger loads",
      "Natural gas removes the refueling problem for long outages",
      "Digital command center display on the panel"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Natural gas hookup needs a licensed plumber and a quick-connect",
      "Natural gas output drops to 8,500 running watts",
      "Heavy, loud and thirsty like the XP13000EH"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for houses already on a natural gas line that want multi-day run time without hauling fuel.",
    "description": "Tri fuel adds natural gas to gasoline and propane, and for houses already plumbed for gas it changes the math of a long outage completely. Instead of swapping cylinders every few hours, a properly installed quick-connect lets the generator run from the utility line for as long as gas pressure holds. DuroMax's spec sheet rates the XP13000HXT at 13,000 peak and 10,500 running watts on gasoline, 12,000 peak and 9,500 running on propane, and 10,000 peak and 8,500 running on natural gas. That natural gas figure is still enough for a large share of a house through a transfer switch. DuroMax lists half-load run time of 8.5 hours on gasoline and 6.5 hours on propane. The HXT adds DuroMax's CO Alert technology, which shuts the engine down if carbon monoxide accumulates, and push-button starting. The caveats: natural gas connection is plumbing work, and DuroMax specifies a supply pressure range that a long run of small pipe may not meet, so have a plumber check it. Retail listings vary on stock, so confirm the exact model number in the listing before you buy."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Dual Fuel Generator",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-dual-fuel-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a dual fuel generator worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For home backup, usually yes. Dual fuel models generally cost only modestly more than gasoline-only versions of the same generator, and they let you store propane, which does not go stale and is often easier to buy after a storm than gasoline. The trade-off is lower output on propane, typically around 5 to 15 percent less than on gasoline, depending on the model."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a dual fuel generator run on a 20 lb propane tank?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on size and load. At half load, Champion lists about 5 hours for its 9,200-watt model, Generac about 5 hours for the GP7500E DF, and Westinghouse about 7 hours for the WGen9500DF. A dual fuel inverter at light load lasts far longer: Westinghouse lists 14 hours for the iGen4500DF at quarter load."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a dual fuel generator produce less power on propane?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Every open-frame model on this list is rated lower on propane. The WGen9500DF goes from 9,500 to 8,500 running watts, the DuroMax XP13000EH from 10,500 to 9,975, and the Generac GP7500E DF from 7,500 to 6,800. Always size your loads against the propane rating if you plan to use propane."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a dual fuel generator on both fuels at once?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The selector chooses one fuel at a time. Some models let you switch while running, others require shutting down first, so read the manual for your model. Most owners run gasoline first, since it ages in the tank, then switch to propane for the rest of a long outage."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between dual fuel and tri fuel generators?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A tri fuel generator adds natural gas to gasoline and propane. With a plumber-installed quick-connect, it can run from your home's gas line without refueling. Output is lowest on natural gas; the DuroMax XP13000HXT is rated at 8,500 running watts on natural gas versus 10,500 on gasoline, and supply pressure must meet the maker's spec."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size dual fuel generator do I need to run a house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For essentials with gas heat, a refrigerator, freezer, sump pump, furnace blower, lights and internet, plan on about 5,000 to 7,000 running watts on propane. A 240V well pump or small central air pushes you toward 9,000 or more. Add the largest starting surge to your running total and keep a margin."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How far away from the house should a propane generator be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The same as a gasoline one. CPSC recommends running portable generators outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust directed away from the home and away from doors, windows and vents. Propane still produces carbon monoxide. Never run any generator in a garage, basement or shed, even with doors open."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "best dual fuel generator",
      "dual fuel generator",
      "propane and gas generator",
      "dual fuel inverter generator",
      "tri fuel generator",
      "propane generator run time"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The Best Dual Fuel Generators for Home Backup
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          Six gas-and-propane generators ranked by what they actually make on propane, how they hook up to a house, and how long a tank lasts.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">11 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Our Dual Fuel Picks</span></a>
