        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Who Should Buy a Dual Fuel Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dual fuel generator runs on gasoline or propane, switching with a dial or a selector valve. For home backup that one feature solves the problem that sinks most outage plans: fuel. Gasoline goes stale in a few months without stabilizer, gas stations need electricity to pump, and after a hurricane or ice storm the lines at the ones that are open can run for hours. Propane keeps for years in a sealed cylinder, and a stack of filled 20 lb tanks in a shed is about the simplest fuel reserve a household can keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our pick for most homes is the <strong>Westinghouse WGen9500DF</strong>. It loses only about a tenth of its output on propane, carries both a 30-amp and a 50-amp outlet for a transfer switch, and starts from a key fob. If you want carbon monoxide shutoff built in and a lower price, the <strong>Champion 9200-Watt Dual Fuel with CO Shield</strong> is the value pick. If you need the most watts on propane, the <strong>DuroMax XP13000EH</strong> rates close to 10,000 running watts on LP. Houses on a natural gas line should look at the tri fuel <strong>DuroMax XP13000HXT</strong>, and anyone who wants quiet over raw output should consider the <strong>Westinghouse iGen4500DF</strong> inverter.
          </p>

          <p>
            This page is a deep dive on one decision inside the bigger question of <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">what kind of home generator to buy for power outages</a>. If you have not yet decided between a standby unit, a portable and a battery station, start there. If you have settled on a portable and want fuel flexibility, read on.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size the generator on its propane rating, not the gasoline headline.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every open-frame dual fuel generator makes less power on propane. If you plan to run on propane during a long outage, the propane running-watt figure is the number your load list has to fit under.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

