        <div id="how-dual-fuel-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Dual Fuel Works, and What Propane Costs You
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dual fuel generator has one engine and two fuel paths. Gasoline flows from the tank through a carburetor as usual. Propane comes from a cylinder through a hose with a regulator, into a mixer or a second carburetor circuit, and the selector tells the engine which one to use. Most models let you switch fuels with the engine stopped; a few allow switching while running. Either way, you are not running both at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane makes less power because it carries less energy per unit of air-fuel mixture than gasoline in a small air-cooled engine. That is why every generator on this list lists lower propane ratings. The gap is the number to compare between models, not a sign that propane is a bad choice. A 9,500-watt gasoline rating that drops to 8,500 on propane is more useful in a long outage than a 10,000-watt rating that drops to 7,500.
          </p>

          <p>
            In exchange for a few hundred watts, propane gives you real advantages. It does not go stale, so a cylinder filled in spring is still good in winter. It does not gum up carburetors, which is the most common reason a generator that sat for a year will not start. It is easier to store safely than gasoline, outdoors, upright and away from ignition sources. And it is often easier to get after a storm, since tank exchange cages and propane dealers keep running when pumps at gas stations are down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane has two drawbacks worth planning for. It burns faster by volume, so a 20 lb cylinder lasts fewer hours than you might expect, as the run-time column shows. And propane pressure falls in very cold weather, which can starve a large engine at high load. Keeping cylinders out of the wind, using a larger tank, and not running a 20 lb cylinder down to empty all help.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical approach most owners settle on: start the outage on gasoline, which is already in the tank, then switch to propane once the gas is gone or the outage looks long. That way the gasoline gets used before it ages, and the propane reserve covers the days when you cannot buy more fuel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Buying Guide: The Specs That Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ignore the biggest number on the box. Generator marketing leads with peak or starting watts on gasoline, which is the least useful number for planning. Here is what to read instead, in order.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane running watts</h3>

          <p>
            Add up the running watts of everything you want on at the same time, then add the largest single starting surge, usually a well pump, sump pump or refrigerator compressor. That total has to fit under the propane running rating with some margin. A refrigerator, a freezer, a furnace blower, a sump pump, lights and internet usually land between 3,000 and 5,000 running watts. Add a window air conditioner and you approach 6,000. A 240V well pump or a small central air system pushes you into the 9,000-watt class.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">120/240V outlets and amperage</h3>

          <p>
            If you want to power circuits in your panel rather than run extension cords, the generator needs a 120/240V locking outlet. An L14-30R supports a 30-amp transfer switch or inlet. A 14-50R supports a 50-amp setup and lets you use more of a large generator's output through the panel. A TT-30R is a 120V RV outlet and does not feed a house panel. Note that some 50-amp outlets are limited below 50 amps by the generator's own breaker; Champion caps the 201302's at 38 amps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CO shutoff</h3>

          <p>
            Champion's CO Shield, Generac's COsense and DuroMax's CO Alert all monitor carbon monoxide near the generator and shut the engine down if it builds up. It is a backup, not permission to run a generator in a garage, but it is a meaningful one and worth paying for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start type, weight and noise</h3>

          <p>
            Electric start with a battery matters more than it seems in a cold, wet outage, and remote start lets you fire it up without walking outside. Every big open-frame unit here weighs 200 lb or more, so check the path from storage to the spot you will run it. Noise ratings around 74 dBA are typical of open frames; inverters are much quieter.
          </p>

