        <div id="transfer-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 30A vs 50A: Connecting a Dual Fuel Generator to the House
          </h2>

          <p>
            A big dual fuel generator earns its keep when it feeds your panel, so the furnace, well pump and hardwired lights work without extension cords snaking through a window. That takes a transfer switch or an interlock kit with a power inlet box, installed by an electrician. It is the only safe way. A so-called suicide cord plugged into a dryer outlet can backfeed the utility lines and kill a lineworker, and it can burn out the generator when power comes back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Matching outlet to switch is straightforward. A generator with an L14-30R pairs with a 30-amp manual transfer switch or a 30-amp inlet, which can deliver up to about 7,200 watts at 240V. A generator with a 14-50R can feed a 50-amp inlet and interlock, which lets a 9,500-watt unit deliver its full output to the panel. The Westinghouse WGen9500DF, the Champion 201302 and both DuroMax units have both outlets, so you can start with a 30-amp setup and step up later. The Generac GP7500E DF has the 30-amp outlet only, which fits its output well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-generator-transfer-switch/">generator transfer switch guide</a> covers the hardware in detail, including which manual switches and inlet boxes match each outlet. If you only need the furnace on backup, a single-circuit furnace transfer switch is a cheaper middle path.
          </p>

          <p>
            One note on inverters: the Westinghouse iGen4500DF is a 120V unit with a TT-30R outlet, and Westinghouse lists it as not transfer switch ready. Use it with heavy-duty extension cords to individual appliances, or for an RV, not as a panel feed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="propane-supply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Planning Your Propane Supply
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run time on propane is the spec most buyers underestimate. The manufacturers on this list put a 20 lb cylinder at roughly 5 to 7 hours at half load for the 7,500 to 10,000-watt class. That means a household running a big generator around the clock could go through three to five standard cylinders a day. Plan accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three common ways to stretch that. The first is simply more 20 lb cylinders, stored outdoors and upright, rotated through an exchange or refill program. The second is a larger cylinder. Many owners move to 30 lb, 40 lb or 100 lb tanks, which need a longer hose and are heavier to move but cut the number of swaps. The third is running the generator in shifts: a few hours in the morning and evening to cool the refrigerator and freezer, charge batteries and run the well pump, then off overnight. A full refrigerator holds a safe temperature for hours with the door closed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shift running is also where a smaller generator shines. The iGen4500DF lists 14 hours on a 20 lb cylinder at quarter load, because its inverter engine throttles down. If your essentials are modest, a quiet inverter running long and light can use less propane than a big open frame working in short bursts.
          </p>

          <p>
            A safety point on storage: never keep propane cylinders indoors, in a basement or in an attached garage. Store them outside, upright, in shade, away from the generator's exhaust and any ignition source, and close the valve when the generator is off. For gasoline, our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">gas generator guide</a> covers stabilizer and safe can storage, which still matters for the half of a dual fuel unit that runs on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tri-fuel-vs-inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Dual Fuel vs Tri Fuel vs Dual Fuel Inverter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three versions of the same idea compete for the same buyer, and the right one depends on your house more than on the generator.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Standard dual fuel open frame</strong> is the default for most homes. It gives the most watts per dollar, both 30-amp and 50-amp outlets on the larger models, and enough output to run most of a house with gas heat. The trade-offs are noise and fuel burn, since the engine runs at a fixed speed regardless of load.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tri fuel</strong> adds natural gas. If your house already has a gas meter, a plumber can install a quick-connect fitting near where the generator will run, and the generator can then run from the utility gas line with no refueling. Natural gas output is lower again, about 8,500 running watts on the DuroMax XP13000HXT against 10,500 on gasoline, and you need adequate supply pressure, but for multi-day outages it is the closest a portable gets to standby convenience. If that appeals, also price a permanent standby unit; our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> compares the two honestly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dual fuel inverter</strong> trades output for quiet and efficiency. The iGen4500DF is rated as low as 52 dBA and stretches propane much further at light loads, and its clean output suits electronics. But it is 120V only and tops out at 3,700 running watts. For a deeper look at inverter generators, including gasoline-only models from Honda, Champion and Generac, see our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generator guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If none of these fit, two alternatives are worth a look. A smaller gasoline inverter covered in our <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">small generator for home guide</a> is lighter and cheaper for essentials-only plans. And a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a> runs indoors with no exhaust, which makes it the right partner to a fuel generator for overnight loads like a CPAP.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Carbon Monoxide Safety and Placement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Propane burns cleaner than gasoline, but a propane-fueled generator still produces carbon monoxide, and enough of it to kill. The U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission estimates that, on average, nearly 80 people die each year from carbon monoxide poisoning caused by portable generators, and outages after storms are when many of those deaths happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            CPSC's guidance is specific. Run portable generators outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from the home and any other building people could enter. Keep them away from doors, windows and vents. Never run one inside a home, a garage, a basement, a crawlspace or a shed, even with doors and windows open, because open windows do not provide enough ventilation to prevent deadly buildup. Some owner's manuals list shorter distances; follow the CPSC's 20-foot guidance anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inside the house, put battery or battery-backup carbon monoxide alarms on every level and outside sleeping areas, and test them before storm season. A CO shutoff on the generator, like CO Shield, COsense or CO Alert, adds a layer, but it monitors the air around the generator, not the air in your bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather is the usual excuse for moving a generator closer to the house. Instead, buy a purpose-made generator cover or tent that allows open-air operation, and set the unit on a dry, level spot. The extra cord length to reach 20 feet is cheap; a heavy-gauge cord or a properly located inlet box solves it. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home outage generator guide</a> has a fuller placement checklist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing on the gasoline rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you buy dual fuel to run on propane, the propane running watts are your real ceiling. A load list that fits the gasoline number can trip the breaker on propane. Every model here loses some output on LP, and the gap varies by brand, so read both columns before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying one 20 lb cylinder and calling it a plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturers rate a 20 lb cylinder at roughly 5 to 7 hours at half load for the big open-frame units. One tank covers an evening, not an outage. Keep several filled cylinders outdoors, or move to a larger tank, and decide in advance whether you will run around the clock or in shifts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord plugged into a dryer or range outlet can energize the utility lines and kill a lineworker, and it can damage the generator when power returns. Use a transfer switch or an interlock with an inlet box, installed by a licensed electrician and matched to the generator's 30A or 50A outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in the garage with the door open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage door does not clear carbon monoxide fast enough. CPSC says to run generators outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with exhaust pointed away and away from doors, windows and vents. A CO shutoff on the generator is a backup, not a license to cut that distance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing propane cylinders indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Propane cylinders belong outside, upright and in the shade, never in a basement, a living space or an attached garage. Close the valve when the generator is off, disconnect the hose for storage, and check the regulator hose for cracks before each season. Leaks indoors can collect at floor level.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

