        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dual fuel generator worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For home backup, usually yes. Dual fuel models generally cost only modestly more than gasoline-only versions of the same generator, and they let you store propane, which does not go stale and is often easier to buy after a storm than gasoline. The trade-off is lower output on propane, typically around 5 to 15 percent less than on gasoline, depending on the model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a dual fuel generator run on a 20 lb propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on size and load. At half load, Champion lists about 5 hours for its 9,200-watt model, Generac about 5 hours for the GP7500E DF, and Westinghouse about 7 hours for the WGen9500DF. A dual fuel inverter at light load lasts far longer: Westinghouse lists 14 hours for the iGen4500DF at quarter load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dual fuel generator produce less power on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every open-frame model on this list is rated lower on propane. The WGen9500DF goes from 9,500 to 8,500 running watts, the DuroMax XP13000EH from 10,500 to 9,975, and the Generac GP7500E DF from 7,500 to 6,800. Always size your loads against the propane rating if you plan to use propane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a dual fuel generator on both fuels at once?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The selector chooses one fuel at a time. Some models let you switch while running, others require shutting down first, so read the manual for your model. Most owners run gasoline first, since it ages in the tank, then switch to propane for the rest of a long outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between dual fuel and tri fuel generators?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A tri fuel generator adds natural gas to gasoline and propane. With a plumber-installed quick-connect, it can run from your home's gas line without refueling. Output is lowest on natural gas; the DuroMax XP13000HXT is rated at 8,500 running watts on natural gas versus 10,500 on gasoline, and supply pressure must meet the maker's spec.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dual fuel generator do I need to run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For essentials with gas heat, a refrigerator, freezer, sump pump, furnace blower, lights and internet, plan on about 5,000 to 7,000 running watts on propane. A 240V well pump or small central air pushes you toward 9,000 or more. Add the largest starting surge to your running total and keep a margin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far away from the house should a propane generator be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The same as a gasoline one. CPSC recommends running portable generators outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust directed away from the home and away from doors, windows and vents. Propane still produces carbon monoxide. Never run any generator in a garage, basement or shed, even with doors open.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

